A||N||6||87ECO5||17" rims, stock brake rotors and factory ride height||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||03-23-2001||10:08 AM||||In about two weeks, I'll be installing a set of 17" Konig Monsoon rims on my '87 economy coupe.  My pals (I won't mention names because <B>fiero56</B> and <B>william_thorniii</B> might get angry ... OOOOPS!!!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   ) are telling me that the looks of the vehicle will be bad if I keep the stock brake rotors and ride height (too much open area between tire and rear quarter lip, and empty space inside rim that should be filled by rotor).<P>Can someone post (or email me) photos of cars matching this description (see thread title)?  I just discovered a leaking rear brake caliper two night ago and, since I need to have that fixed before I install the new rims/tires, I want to know if I should just purchase stock replacement brake parts or scrounge for the money to by an upgrade kit (larger rotors).<P>TIA<P>BTW, here's a shot of the Konig Monsoons:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/konigmonsoon_lg.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||03-23-2001||10:37 AM||||Lots of money to be spent here.....<P>I have seen good results from suspension Techniques lowering springs and 17" wheels. if you want to spend more you can get a set of ground control coil overs. The ground control coil overs are $199 directly from ground control. The suspension Techniques lowering springs are $188 from Discount tire direct (Fierolisa got some a few months ago)<P>That takes care of the height. I have ground control coil overs on the back of my czar , mounted on a set of Koni's , they work great , used to have a set of Fiero store lowering springs they were too soft for what I wanted them for (Autocrossing track events)<P>As for the brakes.. There are a lot of high dollar brake conversions out there<BR> <A HREF="http://www.movit.de" TARGET=_blank>www.movit.de</A>  sells Porsche big reds for the front of a Fiero , there $2700 US last I checked.<P>I'm sure lots of people will post about Grand am conversions , I don't know much about that conversion. I'm doing an 11.26" Chrysler Lebaron conversion all around , with Willwood 4 piston calipers up front , braided lines all around , Blazer mAster cylinder..ect..ect.<P>Here's a link for the info I have been using<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/walt/walt.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/walt/walt.htm</A> <P>this may be a bit excessive I haven't figured out the cost yet. The Front Calipers are $150 each , you can get the std Dynalite units for $99 from summit. mIne are geared a bit more towards racing. I picked up a set of Hubs  (you need them for most brake conversions) For $125 ..thats pretty cheap I think , incls shipping From US to Canada.<P>All in all this will fill up the wheels nicely and put you on par with a mid 90's 911 from what I have heard from a few fiero racers. <P><BR>Hope this helps.<P>JM<BR>86 GT ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||03-23-2001||10:50 AM||||You realy need to lower the car for it to look good with 17s. For me that's a must. Regarding the calipers I do not agree that stock will look bad at all. I just put some MAS President wheels which has openings equal or bigger than the Monsoon. I painted the calipers red and they look awesome. For size I think they look ok. Not bad at all. Mine is an 88 and I don't know if pre 88 calipers are smaller. I wish I could have a photo to show you. Maybe next week (too busy with Daytona) I will have some. Email me if you are realy desperate for them.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||03-23-2001||11:02 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerfiero3.jpg" TARGET=_blank>Is this what you mean?</A>      <P>A brake upgrade probably won't happen till after something is done with the engine.  It will be lowered REAL soon.  I'm aware of the stock brakes, does it really bother me?, no... well maybe just a little bit.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Those Monsoons are cool!  IMO, they shouldn't look all that bad with stock brakes.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mr.Frosty||03-23-2001||12:13 PM||||I am going that route soon myself with the 17" wheels and brakes.I am lowering all around,and am going to paint the calipers.As far as stock brakes go,they don't look that bad if you dress them up and keep them clean,But if you drive it everyday or year round there's not much you can do.The midwest salt sucks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> just be thankful our cars came standard with discs all around.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||10
